From 03edf12ba600dd0cf2e40e1704f1a20858f85c78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vsevolod Stakhov <vsevolod@rspamd.com>
Date: Thu, 27 Aug 2026 12:29:44 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [CritFix] rdns: harden DNS reply parser against malformed
 packets

The DNS reply parser consumes fully attacker-controlled bytes, yet it
decoded names, resource records and TCP frames with unchecked or wrapping
arithmetic. This reworks the name decoding around a single bounds-checked
iterator and fixes several boundary defects:

- Question comparison and label parsing now bound every access to the
  packet, cap compression-pointer hops and total name length, and reject
  truncated or reserved labels instead of reading out of bounds.
- Encoded names embedded in RDATA are bound to their RDLENGTH (compression
  pointers may still target the whole packet), and PTR/NS/CNAME/MX/SRV/SOA
  verify exact RDATA consumption so a short or long RDLENGTH cannot shift
  parsing of the following records.
- A records require exactly four bytes; every RR is length-validated and
  resynchronised to its RDATA end.
- TXT chunk accounting uses size_t instead of an 8-bit counter that wrapped
  after 255 segments.
- Partially parsed allocations are freed on every error path and all
  allocations are checked.
- TCP reads drain iteratively with a per-event packet budget instead of
  recursing, and cur_read is widened to uint32_t so a maximal 65535-byte
  DNS-over-TCP frame no longer overflows the 16-bit byte counter.
